WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.920
Hey, Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:07.860
Imagine you’re the captain of a large cruise ship&nbsp;
steaming across the north Atlantic Ocean. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:14.220
a cold night, and you can see nothing but stars&nbsp;
and endless black. Your moustache is twitching,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:19.800
a sure sign you’re feeling uneasy - and not just&nbsp;
because you had a bit too much sherry with dinner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:26.700
Out here in the freezing open ocean, you know&nbsp;
there’s one risk to your ship greater than any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.360
other. And that risk is……?
Flooding.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:37.920
Yes, unsurprisingly, the most common reason ships&nbsp;
sink is because they fill with water. But if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:44.220
fell for my careful priming, the first thing that&nbsp;
popped into your head was probably ‘icebergs.’ Of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:47.940
course, that would have been wrong because there&nbsp;
probably aren’t many icebergs around these days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:54.600
but inappropriate climate change jokes and my&nbsp;
subtle nudging aside, there’s another obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:59.580
reason an iceberg may have bobbed into view in&nbsp;
your mind’s eye just now - because of the sinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:06.060
of the Titanic, by far the most famous maritime&nbsp;
disaster of all time. It’s a tragic story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:11.220
and not just because scientists have recently&nbsp;
proven something we’ve all suspected for a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:16.620
time - that Jack could actually have survived if&nbsp;
he’d just climbed on the damn door next to Rose.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:27.960
A staggering 1,500 souls perished that night when&nbsp;
the unsinkable ship did the unthinkable and sank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.620
but that doesn’t even come close to topping&nbsp;
the list of the deadliest maritime disasters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:40.380
in history. Because there’s something far&nbsp;
scarier than giant ice cubes lurking out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:51.060
there in the ocean - us. What really causes large&nbsp;
scale loss of human life at sea are torpedos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:57.360
shelling, mines, depth charges and,&nbsp;
when all else fails, kamikaze fighters.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:05.520
According to one prominent insurer, in 2020,&nbsp;
a total of 48 ships were lost worldwide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:19.260
By comparison, between 1939 and 1945,14,341 ships&nbsp;
were sunk. That’s more than 2,000 ships per year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:23.580
But even in the company of such morbid statistics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:29.940
the sinking of one ship in particular stands out&nbsp;
for the almost unimaginable scale of its tragedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:36.300
and strangely enough you’ve probably never&nbsp;
even heard of it. Because, for reasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:42.600
which will soon become clear, the story of the&nbsp;
Wilhelm Gustloff has been largely forgotten by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:50.040
history. And to resurrect it, we have&nbsp;
to go back in time to January 1945.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:13.500
The Second World War was nearing its end and&nbsp;
the Russian army was advancing on Europe from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.300
the east, intent on putting an end to&nbsp;
Nazi Germany’s hopes of establishing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:23.640
Third Reich. The Russian army wasn’t exactly&nbsp;
known for its kind and merciful treatment of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:29.580
Germans – military personnel or otherwise&nbsp;
– and so quite possibly the largest mass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:34.920
evacuation in history began on the Eastern&nbsp;
Front under the name of Operation Hannibal.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:40.140
Although a large number of civilians were taken&nbsp;
back to Germany, the number one aim of Operation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:46.380
Hannibal was to bring a large chunk of the&nbsp;
remaining German Army back to the Fatherland to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:54.840
continue the fight. Hannibal lasted for 115 days&nbsp;
in the Spring of 1945, during which time as many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.840 --> 00:05:02.880
as 2 million people were evacuated. By contrast,&nbsp;
the famous Operation Dynamo that saw the rescue of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:11.580
338,000 Allied troops from the beaches of Dunkirk&nbsp;
had taken just nine days in May and June of 1940.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:16.020
During the evacuation of Dunkirk,&nbsp;
every available ship or boat was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:21.060
drafted in for the rescue of troops, and&nbsp;
the same was true of Operation Hannibal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:28.380
But there was certainly a discrepancy in&nbsp;
scale. Dunkirk saw about 235 boats sunk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:34.260
and an estimated 5,000 soldiers lost during&nbsp;
the evacuation. During Operation Hannibal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:41.340
only 10 more ships – a total of 245&nbsp;
– were lost, but there were more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:50.820
33,000 fatalities. And almost a third of those&nbsp;
belonged to just one ship - the Wilhelm Gustloff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:57.240
a huge vessel that had originally been&nbsp;
commissioned by the Nazi Party as a cruise liner.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:04.620
On the 30th of January 1945, the Wilhelm Gustloff&nbsp;
was sunk in the Baltic Sea by a Soviet torpedo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:11.100
The Gustloff was carrying Germans from occupied&nbsp;
countries like Lithuania, Estonia, Latvia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.980
and East Prussia, and was headed for Gdynia in&nbsp;
German-occupied Poland. Though there were also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:19.920
military personnel aboard, the passengers were&nbsp;
mainly civilians. By most standards these were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:25.500
innocent people, but this was the tail end of&nbsp;
the biggest war in history, and the innocence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:33.480
German civilians wasn’t quite so straightforward&nbsp;
in the eyes of many as it might have been. Fair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:40.620
or not, that judgement made sure the incredible&nbsp;
tragedy of the sinking of the Wilhelm Gustloff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:48.360
very probably the greatest maritime disaster&nbsp;
ever, never made it into the history books.

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:52.260
At over 200 metres long and weighing more than 25&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:58.920
thousand tons, the Gustloff was launched&nbsp;
on the 5th of May 1937 to great fanfare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:04.740
Being the centrepiece of the KdF fleet, it was&nbsp;
originally going to be called the ‘Adolf Hitler.’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:12.600
(The KdF, which stood for Kraft durch Freude&nbsp;
or ‘Strength through Joy,’ was a state run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:20.280
leisure organisation set up in 1933 to reward&nbsp;
the German people for good behaviour by giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:28.200
them access to fun activities. You could say&nbsp;
the KdF put the Party into ‘Nazi Party...’)

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:33.540
In the end, Wilhelm Gustloff’s name was&nbsp;
chosen for the ship - he’d been the leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:39.060
of the Nazi Party in Switzerland before being&nbsp;
assassinated by a Jewish medical student in 1936.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:45.240
After sitting next to Gustloff’s widow at the&nbsp;
memorial service, Hitler decided on the name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:50.700
change in Wilhelm’s honour. Frau Gustloff was&nbsp;
even invited to officially launch the ship.  
&nbsp;

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:58.620
Before and during the war, devoted workers&nbsp;
and Nazi officials were rewarded with cruises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:04.560
and concerts aboard the Gustloff. On&nbsp;
its unofficial maiden voyage in 1938,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:10.980
the ship took Austrians on a four-day cruise in a&nbsp;
not-so-subtle attempt to get them to vote for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:17.880
annexation of Austria by Germany, a neat little&nbsp;
manouver known today as the Anschluss. In reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:22.800
the Austrian populace were being threatened with&nbsp;
a swift death for voting against annexation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:28.200
so being bribed with champagne and&nbsp;
seasickness probably wasn’t necessary.

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:38.040
The Gustloff took over 80,000 passengers on&nbsp;
pleasure cruises between March 1938 and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:43.140
outbreak of the war in September 1939, at&nbsp;
which time it was recommissioned for use as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:48.840
a hospital ship for the first year of the war.&nbsp;
Towards the end of 1940, things changed again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:54.240
All the medical equipment was removed and the&nbsp;
Gustloff became a barracks ship for submariner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:59.880
trainees in Poland. Not much happened for the&nbsp;
ship and its crew for the next four years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:07.920
but in 1945 it was perfectly placed to help in&nbsp;
the evacuation efforts of Operation Hannibal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:12.480
and it was converted again,&nbsp;
this time into an escape boat.

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:18.960
By the time the operation began, most of the&nbsp;
available ships were in fairly poor condition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:25.080
and the Allied Forces had a firm grip on the&nbsp;
skies, while the Baltic Sea was heavily mined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:29.940
and teeming with Russian submarines.&nbsp;
Oh, and despite what I said earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:35.880
it was a brutally cold winter so icebergs&nbsp;
were actually a genuine danger too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:42.300
All in all, the deck was heavily&nbsp;
stacked against the fleeing Germans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:47.640
and it was the passengers on the Wilhelm&nbsp;
Gustloff that paid the maximum price.

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:55.260
On the 30th of January 1945, the port of Gdynia&nbsp;
was full of thousands of refugees, including women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.260 --> 00:10:01.860
and children, soldiers, Nazi Officials, and U-Boat&nbsp;
trainees, all desperate to get away. The Gustloff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:06.900
was the largest ship available, but it had only&nbsp;
been designed to carry 1,900 people, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:13.800
its crew. By the time it set sail, there were more&nbsp;
than five times that number on board. Some people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:20.640
had tickets, but most simply forced their way&nbsp;
onto the ship, and who could blame them? When the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:26.880
options are escape or death, you’re going to give&nbsp;
escape a go no matter how dangerous it might be.

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:32.040
According to records, there were&nbsp;
6,000 passengers and crew on the ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:38.640
but the real number is thought to be upwards&nbsp;
of 10,000, 90% of whom were civilians.

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:44.460
When the ship left port just after midday, it&nbsp;
was hugely overcrowded with people occupying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:50.400
every spare space they could find, including&nbsp;
the long empty swimming pool. But nobody was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:55.980
working on their tan – records say it was snowing&nbsp;
that day, and the mercury was at 10 degrees below&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.980 --> 00:11:03.360
freezing. Inside the ship, the sheer number of&nbsp;
people ensured the atmosphere was hot and humid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:08.100
and many resorted to removing their&nbsp;
life-jackets in an attempt to cool down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:15.420
Yeah, in hindsight that wasn’t ideal,&nbsp;
but then again, a bit of extra buoyancy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:19.860
wouldn’t have made all that much difference&nbsp;
in the freezing waters of the Baltic sea.

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:25.440
In what sounds like the start of a bad joke, there&nbsp;
were four captains onboard the ship, and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:30.840
couldn’t agree on the safest route to take. Some&nbsp;
wanted to stick to the shallower waters along the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:36.300
coast to minimise the chance of encountering&nbsp;
submarines, while others thought the deeper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:42.300
mine-swept waters further out to sea were their&nbsp;
best bet. In the end, they opted for the latter.

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:48.360
At around 6pm, a German mine-sweeping&nbsp;
convoy was spotted in the area and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:52.260
to avoid any sort of collision, one of&nbsp;
the captains ordered that the Gustloff’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:56.940
red and green navigation lights be turned&nbsp;
on, lighting the ship up like a floating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.940 --> 00:12:03.540
Christmas tree. By 7pm the Gustloff was being&nbsp;
tailed by a Soviet submarine called S-13.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:11.160
Even though a torpedo boat was accompanying&nbsp;
the Gustloff to protect her against subs, its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:17.640
submarine detector and anti-aircraft guns had been&nbsp;
frozen, leaving the vessels totally unprotected.

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:24.360
S-13 reeled in the Gustloff during a two&nbsp;
hour chase before firing four torpedoes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:29.340
There are rumours that each of the torpedoes&nbsp;
had been adorned with a hand painted message,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:34.440
and even though the fourth missile got&nbsp;
wedged in the submarine’s torpedo tube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:42.480
the other three successfully delivered their&nbsp;
mail. Within an hour the Gustloff had sunk.
&nbsp;

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:49.620
Dozens of people were killed instantly by&nbsp;
the blasts, and thousands more were drowned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:54.780
trapped below decks. Those who made for the&nbsp;
lifeboats had to navigate a stampede in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.780 --> 00:13:00.240
panicked passengers were crushed to death. All&nbsp;
but nine of the lifeboats were frozen in place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:04.620
and even those that were broken free were&nbsp;
difficult to release because one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:10.140
torpedoes had hit the crew’s quarters, killing&nbsp;
the only people who knew how to operate them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:15.780
Still, several lifeboats did start getting&nbsp;
lowered towards the sea, but about twenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:21.300
minutes after the first torpedo found its mark,&nbsp;
the ship suddenly listed violently to port.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:26.220
The lifeboats that were being lowered on the&nbsp;
opposite side of the ship were smashed into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:32.520
the Gustloff’s hull, destroying them and casting&nbsp;
their occupants into the freezing water below.

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:40.320
The few lifeboats that did make it down safely&nbsp;
were soon faced with a new threat - boarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:44.940
from the hundreds of swimmers already&nbsp;
in the water. Eyewitness accounts speak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:49.980
of desperate men and women trying to haul&nbsp;
themselves aboard the lifeboats while the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:55.620
current occupants beat them back with fists,&nbsp;
oars, and some choice German swear words.

00:13:56.760 --> 00:14:00.960
Support boats did eventually arrive, and&nbsp;
between them they were able to rescue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:08.040
about 1,250 people through the night.&nbsp;
They were the very, very lucky ones.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:16.980
Nobody knows exactly how many people died that&nbsp;
night, but most estimates put the figure at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:24.540
about 9,400 souls – that’s more than six&nbsp;
Titanics, or three September the 11ths.

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:30.480
The last survivors were found at dawn&nbsp;
when a German naval officer climbed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:36.120
into a drifting lifeboat to check for&nbsp;
signs of life. At first he found none,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:42.060
but among the bodies was a baby that&nbsp;
had been tightly wrapped in blankets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:50.100
It was still alive. The same officer would go&nbsp;
on to adopt and raise the child as his own in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.100 --> 00:14:57.720
what was one of the few tiny bright points on&nbsp;
one of the blackest nights in maritime history.

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:02.880
Thanks for watching.

